# NODX 1.0 Signature Workflow {#title}

NODX 1.0 verifies signatures over the **frozen canonical AST contract**. CST
trivia (whitespace, attribute order, comments preserved by the editor profile)
never affects the digest, so two byte-different sources with identical AST
yield the same digest.

::::section {#digest}
## Digest

The canonical digest is `sha256-BASE64URL_WITHOUT_PADDING` over the canonical
JSON serialization produced by `nodx ast file.nodx`:

:::code {lang="sh"}
nodx ast doc.nodx | shasum -a 256
:::

In Rust:

:::code {lang="rust"}
use nodx_core::{canonical_json, parse_bytes, sha256_base64url};
let bytes = std::fs::read("doc.nodx")?;
let doc = parse_bytes(&bytes)?;
let digest = sha256_base64url(canonical_json(&doc).as_bytes());
:::
::::

::::section {#jws}
## JWS verification

`nodx-sign` accepts detached and packaged JWS signatures with **ES256
mandatory**. The protected header rejects `crit`, `jwk`, `jku`, `x5u`, `x5c`,
`x5t`, and `x5t#S256` smuggling, and enforces `signature_header_bytes` from
`ResourceLimits` to prevent header DoS.

:::table {caption="Verifier policy at a glance"}
